WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.886 --> 00:00:07.086
What you're seeing here is a key moment
for the gene editing tool CRISPR-Cas9.

00:00:07.086 --> 00:00:11.156
That's because the doctors are injecting
a patient with an experimental drug

00:00:11.156 --> 00:00:15.086
that will edit their DNA inside their body.

00:00:15.086 --> 00:00:20.046
CRISPR-Cas9 holds a lot of promise,
but up till now, all the treatments

00:00:20.046 --> 00:00:23.046
in human clinical trials have been ex vivo.

00:00:23.046 --> 00:00:27.956
They have relied on taking cells out
of the body in order to edit them.

00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:33.026
Like in a treatment for sickle cell anemia,
researchers removed cells from the bone marrow

00:00:33.026 --> 00:00:38.516
of a young woman in Mississippi, modified them
to turn on the production of fetal hemoglobin,

00:00:38.606 --> 00:00:40.706
and then infused the cells back in.

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:45.956
But for many inherited diseases,
like brain, muscle or kidney disease,

00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:50.266
you just don't have cells that
you can take out and put back in.

00:00:50.266 --> 00:00:55.476
Which is why the first in vivo trial,
called BRILLIANCE, is so important.

00:00:55.476 --> 00:01:02.116
So let's take a look at how the
in vivo gene editing drug works.

00:01:02.116 --> 00:01:05.856
CRISPR-Cas9 technology is
adapted from bacteria and archae,

00:01:06.186 --> 00:01:10.606
specifically from a defense system
used to detect and destroy phages.

00:01:11.356 --> 00:01:16.686
And it's really appealing to researchers because
it's cheap, precise, and highly adaptable.

00:01:17.316 --> 00:01:18.676
It's also elegant.

00:01:19.226 --> 00:01:24.046
Its basic principles are fairly straightforward,
although there are many modifications of this

00:01:24.046 --> 00:01:27.006
and many layers of additional
technologies now being deployed.

00:01:27.446 --> 00:01:33.376
But at its core it's a combination of the Cas9
enzyme, which is really good at cutting DNA,

00:01:33.376 --> 00:01:39.686
and a guide RNA which directs the
Cas9 enzyme to a specific location

00:01:39.686 --> 00:01:44.816
in the genome This guide RNA is designed
to be unique to each application.

00:01:44.816 --> 00:01:49.466
It contains a 20 base pair sequence
called a "spacer region" that's designed

00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:53.796
to be a homologous base pair
match to the cell's DNA.

00:01:53.796 --> 00:01:56.396
As this whole complex bounces around the cell,

00:01:56.396 --> 00:02:01.506
the Cas9 attaches to the cell's double
helix at short sequences called PAM.

00:02:01.506 --> 00:02:05.446
The specific PAM sequence depends
on the Cas enzyme being used.

00:02:05.446 --> 00:02:09.296
Each time this unwinds the
double helix just a little bit,

00:02:09.636 --> 00:02:12.236
allowing the guide RNA to
check if there's a match.

00:02:12.236 --> 00:02:14.676
If not, the complex moves off.

00:02:16.046 --> 00:02:22.616
But if those base pairs do match, the guide
RNA will continue to anneal to the target DNA.

00:02:22.616 --> 00:02:27.416
Once the Cas9 detects that every base
pair of the spacer region is a match

00:02:27.416 --> 00:02:30.836
to the cell's DNA, it cleaves the double helix.

00:02:30.906 --> 00:02:36.476
And this by itself can be enough
to knockout, or disable, the gene.

00:02:36.476 --> 00:02:39.676
Scientists can also provide a
repair template to the cell.

00:02:39.676 --> 00:02:43.626
And this is the most basic
way this technology works,

00:02:43.726 --> 00:02:49.196
but CRISPR-Cas systems are being rapidly
developed, so it can also do lots more.

00:02:49.866 --> 00:02:53.726
Now let's take a look at a
CRISPR-Cas9 treatment in practice.

00:02:54.226 --> 00:03:00.096
The in vivo trial I mentioned earlier is testing
a drug for Leber congenital amaurosis type 10,

00:03:00.426 --> 00:03:03.236
a disease that affects photoreceptor
cells in the retina

00:03:03.446 --> 00:03:06.036
and causes severe, early-onset vision loss.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:10.996
To get the drug into the eye, doctors
performed a sub-retinal injection.

00:03:11.376 --> 00:03:19.916
So the procedure itself involves a surgery
to perform that subretinal injection

00:03:19.916 --> 00:03:23.336
where the surgeon makes three
tiny incisions in the eye

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:27.706
so they can put instruments inside
the eye to remove the vitreous gel,

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:31.846
which is needed so that the
very fine cannula that's used

00:03:32.186 --> 00:03:37.936
to make the subretinal injection can be
passed across the eye, placed very delicately

00:03:37.936 --> 00:03:41.776
under the retina to inject this
small amount of gene editing drug.

00:03:41.776 --> 00:03:44.196
That drug contains an adeno-associated
virus vector.

00:03:44.286 --> 00:03:47.536
This virus is the delivery
system for CRISPR-Cas9.

00:03:47.606 --> 00:03:50.836
So the researchers put the
sequence encoding the Cas9 protein

00:03:51.056 --> 00:03:56.696
and two guide RNAs inside the virus, and the
virus carries it like cargo to the cells.

00:03:56.696 --> 00:04:01.366
The target is a mutation in
photoreceptor cells, in the CEP290 gene.

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:05.866
So the most common mutation in
the CEP290 gene that's associated

00:04:06.086 --> 00:04:12.506
with severe early onset vision loss is an
intronic mutation that results in the inclusion

00:04:12.506 --> 00:04:19.546
of a cryptic exon, an abnormal exon in
the gene so the gene doesn't function.

00:04:19.636 --> 00:04:26.206
Introns are noncoding parts of sequences
that get removed during RNA splicing.

00:04:26.446 --> 00:04:27.436
Exons get left in.

00:04:27.436 --> 00:04:31.446
And that one wrong letter means that
not all of the intron gets removed.

00:04:31.446 --> 00:04:37.276
So it's a great target for early
CRISPR therapeutic because all we need

00:04:37.716 --> 00:04:40.666
to do is cut out the mutation in the intron.

00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:43.386
And that, the researchers hope, will
restore gene function to the cells.

00:04:43.826 --> 00:04:48.676
They're looking to enroll 18 patients, with
results starting to trickle in in 2021.

00:04:48.676 --> 00:04:52.776
But while they're gonna be paying
close attention to the efficacy

00:04:52.916 --> 00:04:56.966
of the medicine, the primary outcome is safety.

00:04:57.026 --> 00:05:02.916
And a lot of uncertainty still remains
about CRISPR-Cas systems more generally.

00:05:03.216 --> 00:05:16.026
But one thing is clear: we are
finally at a point where the promise

00:05:16.026 --> 00:05:19.646
of CRISPR-Cas9 is beginning to
be tested out in the real world.

